WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
Secure password storage in Python means passwords are never stored in plaintext or reversible form.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.040
The application should not be able to recover a user's password.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:17.580
It should only be able to verify a submitted password against a stored verifier created with a vetted password hashing approach.

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:25.040
This design reduces the harm if a password database, backup, export, or development copy is exposed.

5
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:36.600
Strong designs use mature password hashing libraries, per-password salts, appropriate work factors, safe secret handling, protected recovery flows, migration planning, and careful logging.

6
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.580
Salts provide uniqueness, not secrecy.

7
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.920
Peppers, if used, are shared secrets that require serious operational planning.

8
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:53.300
Work factors must balance offline attack resistance with user experience, server capacity, and availability.

9
00:00:53.580 --> 00:01:03.260
Password storage is connected to authentication architecture, account recovery, session management, monitoring, incident response, and user trust.

10
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.120
Password reset flows must be protected as carefully as login.

11
00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:10.100
Password changes may need session effects.

12
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.080
Legacy hashes need migration plans.

13
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:19.500
Authentication logs should support defense without recording credentials or reset secrets.

14
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.920
Abuse monitoring helps identify when password controls are being stressed.

15
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:27.000
The goal is not to make passwords invincible.

16
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:34.300
The goal is to reduce the damage caused by password database exposure and make authentication systems safer to operate.

17
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:51.760
Durable password security comes from simple, reviewed implementation, vetted libraries, explicit parameters, safe reset workflows, tested migrations, privacy-aware logs, and continuous improvement as technology and threat patterns change.
